why 1 3/8 RANGE The bass climbs a whole scale, G–A–B–C♯–D, to a high D. A sweeping bass line like this is typical of Bach.
why 1 1/2 DOUBLING Alto (G–F♯) and tenor (E–F♯) meet on F♯ from opposite sides, by step. The bass is busy with its climb, and the inner voices take the nearest notes.
why 1 1/2 RANGE The top of the bass's climb, high for a bass. Bach's singers could reach it; in exercises, keep the bass lower.
why 1 3/4 RANGE The bass starts down from its peak, C♯–B, so the high stretch lasts little more than a beat.
why 5 0 SEVENTH The alto's seventh E rises to F♯ instead of falling to D. The soprano already takes D, so falling would double the third and leave out the fifth.
why 5 1/4 LEAPS The tenor resolves A♯ to B and then leaps a fourth to E. The tritone A♯–E spans two different chords, so it is not heard as one interval.
why 6 0 SIMILAR_MOTION Every voice falls by step, and the bass by a fifth, into iv⁶. The phrase is descending to its cadence; contrary motion returns on the next beat.
why 8 1/4 CROSSING The alto drops to the leading tone A♯ to resolve it up to B at the fermata, while the tenor holds C♯ above it. A brief crossing so that each voice gets the note it needs.
why 11 3/4 OVERLAP The melody dips low, down to D, so the alto rising B–E passes where the soprano was. The soprano leaps up to G at the same moment, so the order stays clear.
why 12 3/8 LEAPS The tenor leaps down a diminished fifth, C–F♯, and then up to B, inside the leap. Coming back inside is what the rule asks; Bach does it by a fourth instead of a step.
